      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill">X22i</a>
          is the obvious point of comparison, since it's the same motor,
          deck, and incline range wrapped around a considerably larger
          22-inch rotating screen instead of the X9i's 10-inch pivoting one.
          Unless the display itself is a priority, we think the X9i is the
          smarter buy for most shoppers in this category — you're not
          giving up any incline performance to save the money. The
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          is a useful reference point in the other direction: a flat
          treadmill with a 12% max incline, meaningfully cheaper than either
          incline trainer, and the right choice if you've read this far and
          realized you don't actually need 40% incline range in your
          training.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth naming the trade-off plainly: you're saving several
          hundred dollars by accepting a smaller, less rotatable screen, not
          by accepting a lesser workout. For shoppers whose budget is the
          deciding factor between an incline trainer and settling for a flat
          treadmill's limited incline, the X9i is arguably the better
          recommendation of the two X-Series machines, because it gets more
          people into the category who might otherwise talk themselves out
          of it on price alone.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
