        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          As much as we like the X9i, it isn't the right machine for every
          shopper. If display size and immersive trail video genuinely
          matter to you, the smaller screen will be a persistent annoyance,
          and the <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill">X22i</a>
          is worth the extra money instead. If you mostly want a
          comfortable flat run or light incline walk, you're paying for
          incline range you'll rarely use — a
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          serves that use case better and for less. And if you know you'll
          never subscribe to iFit, the terrain-matched coaching that
          justifies this category of machine goes unused, making a simpler
          treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews">home treadmill guide</a>
          the more sensible purchase.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack X9i is the incline trainer we'd recommend to most
          budget-conscious hikers, trail runners, and incline-focused HIIT
          trainees, because it delivers the identical -6% to 40% incline
          range, the same 4.0 CHP motor, and the same true decline as the
          considerably pricier X22i. The smaller 10-inch screen is a real
          trade-off during immersive trail workouts, but it doesn't change
          what your legs experience during a climb, and that's ultimately
          what this category of machine is for.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're deciding between the X9i and the X22i, our advice is
          simple: pay for the incline hardware first, and only pay extra for
          the screen if you know display size and rotation genuinely matter
          to how you'll use the machine. For most shoppers, that extra money
          is better saved or put toward a longer iFit subscription instead.
        </p>
