        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the real difference between the X9i and the X22i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The core hardware is identical: both use a 4.0 CHP motor, a 22
              x 60-inch deck, and the same -6% to 40% incline and decline
              range. The difference is the display. The X9i has a 10-inch
              pivoting touchscreen while the X22i steps up to a 22-inch
              rotating touchscreen. If you care mainly about the incline
              training itself, the X9i gives you essentially the same
              workout for several hundred dollars less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 10-inch screen too small for iFit trail workouts?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It's noticeably smaller than the X22i's 22-inch display, and
              trail video is less immersive as a result, but it's not too
              small to use comfortably. Our testers had no trouble reading
              workout stats, following trainer instructions, or watching
              trail footage during coached sessions; it simply feels more
              like watching a tablet than a window into the trail compared
              to the larger screen.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need iFit to use the X9i's incline features?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              You can manually adjust incline and decline without a
              subscription, but the automatic terrain-matched incline
              changes during real trail footage require an active iFit
              membership, exactly as on the X22i. Without it, the X9i still
              functions as a capable manual incline machine, but its
              signature coached-trail feature goes unused.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who should buy the X9i instead of the X22i?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Anyone who wants the full -6% to 40% incline training
              experience but doesn't need or want to pay for a larger
              rotating display. Budget-conscious hikers and trail runners,
              or anyone who primarily uses a phone or tablet on a stand for
              entertainment during workouts anyway, will get essentially the
              same physical training benefit from the X9i for meaningfully
              less money.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is the X9i during steep incline changes?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The incline motor is audible during rapid grade changes,
              comparable to the X22i since both share the same incline
              hardware. It's noticeably louder during a shift than a flat
              treadmill's incline motor simply because it's moving the deck
              through a much wider angle, but it settles down once the grade
              stabilizes and isn't disruptive during normal use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Plan for roughly 80 minutes working alone, or about 50 minutes
              with a second person, slightly faster than the X22i thanks to
              the smaller, lighter console. Two people are still recommended
              for lifting the upright frame into place, and a full incline
              calibration cycle should be run before your first workout.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
